Thought: The ranking is unstable and several models are highly correlated, so a robust median over the most consistent models should outperform weighted or mean approaches.  
Action: select_stable  
Action Input: {"k":5,"metric":"rmse"}

Thought: The ranking is unstable and many models are correlated; a robust median over the most stable subset (pool1) should outperform weighted or mean approaches.  
Action: evaluate_strategy  
Action Input: {"combine":"median","pool":"pool1","rationale":"robust median on the stable pool"}

Thought: Try an error‑based weighted average on the reduced, decorrelated pool (pool4) to see if the inverse‑error weighting beats the robust median on pool1.  
Action: evaluate_strategy  
Action Input: {"combine":"weighted","pool":"pool4","weights":"w3","rationale":"inverse‑error weighting on decorrelated pool"}

Thought: The ranking is unstable and errors are correlated, but the best model changes across horizons. A per‑horizon best‑model weighting may capture that variation and improve over the robust median.  
Action: weights_best_per_horizon  
Action Input: {"pool":"pool1","metric":"rmse"}

Thought: The current best is a trimmed‑mean on a stable pool; try a weighted combination that shrinks the inverse‑error weights toward uniform to reduce variance from only 3 windows.  
Action: weights_inverse_error  
Action Input: {"pool":"pool1","metric":"rmse","shrinkage":0.5,"per_horizon":false}

Thought: Test inverse‑error weighting on the stable pool with no shrinkage to see if a pure error‑based mix beats the trimmed mean.
Action: weights_inverse_error
Action Input: {"pool":"pool1","metric":"rmse","shrinkage":0.0,"per_horizon":false}

Thought: The ranking is unstable and errors are highly correlated; a forward‑selection weighted combination on the stable pool may capture complementary strengths better than simple inverse‑error weights.  
Action: weights_forward  
Action Input: {"pool":"pool1","max_steps":20,"metric":"rmse"}

Thought: The current best uses trimmed_mean on pool1. To explore a different robust approach, prune highly correlated models first, then apply inverse‑error weighting on the decorrelated subset.  
Action: prune_redundant  
Action Input: {"pool":"pool_full","corr_threshold":0.95,"metric":"rmse"}

Thought: The ranking is unstable and several models are highly correlated; a weighted scheme that adapts to error trends across horizons may capture complementary strengths better than a static inverse‑error or forward‑selection weight.  
Action: weights_error_trend  
Action Input: {"pool":"pool1","metric":"mae","eta":1.0,"damping":null}

Thought: Test a simple inverse‑error weighted combination on the stable pool1 to see if it beats the trimmed mean leader.  
Action: evaluate_strategy  
Action Input: {"combine":"weighted","pool":"pool1","weights":"w5","rationale":"inverse‑error weighting on the stable pool"}

Thought: Evaluate the inverse‑error weighting on the stable pool with no shrinkage (w2) to see if it improves over the trimmed‑mean leader.  
Action: evaluate_strategy  
Action Input: {"combine":"weighted","pool":"pool1","weights":"w2","rationale":"inverse‑error weighting without shrinkage on the stable pool"}

Thought: The current best strategy is a5 (trimmed_mean on pool1 trim=0.2) with the lowest score; no better candidate remains.  
Action: accept  
Action Input: {}

